XMA streams that interleave packets (packet skip count above 0) continue into the other input buffer at the packet the
skip count names, not at its first packet. The frame that crosses the buffer boundary took its tail from packet 0, which
belongs to another stream, and the stream resumed there; FFmpeg then rejected the frame ("would have to skip N bits",
"invalid scale factor coding", "overflow in spectral RLE", "invalid number of fill bits"). A skip that reaches a buffer
the guest has not refilled yet is applied when it is, unless the guest places the read offset itself.

Also: the search for the next frame looks through every packet of both buffers and accepts a frame that begins in a
packet's last 32 bits; a frame whose header is split across packets is counted, so the frame before it is not taken as
the packet's last; a frame whose header or tail is in a buffer the guest has not refilled waits for it instead of being
dropped or setting error 4.

The same invariant is fixed in the ReXGlue SDK tree of codepdbh/nfsmw-android (sdk/, BSD-3-Clause like the SDK).

